4. WHAT IS FLAT STRUCTURE?
============================================================

Original CitiML XPath:

citimlTradeNotification/
citimlNotificationBundleDetails/
citimlEvents/
citimlEvent/
nonpublicExecutionReport/
ns1:termination/
ns1:effectiveDate

Original hierarchical structure:

citimlTradeNotification
    |
    +-- citimlNotificationBundleDetails
            |
            +-- citimlEvents
                    |
                    +-- citimlEvent
                            |
                            +-- nonpublicExecutionReport
                                    |
                                    +-- termination
                                            |
                                            +-- effectiveDate

Each child element is nested inside its parent.

Current flat Data Catalog structure:

Dictionary: CitiMLTest

    Collection: CITIMLTRADENOTIFICATION
    Collection: CITIMLNOTIFICATIONBUNDLEDETAILS
    Collection: CITIMLEVENTS

All collections are registered at the same level under
the dictionary.

They are not physically nested inside each other.

Without explicit relationships, the flat structure
does not show which collection is the parent or child.

Our current workaround:

Create flat collections.
Create self attributes under each collection.
Create attribute-to-attribute lineage relationships.

For example:

CITIMLTRADENOTIFICATION
    |
    | LINEAGE
    v
CITIMLNOTIFICATIONBUNDLEDETAILS
    |
    | LINEAGE
    v
CITIMLEVENTS

The collections remain flat, but their relationships
describe the original hierarchy.

Important:

The Dictionary API Swagger description mentions that
concepts can be nested.

However, native hierarchical support for our specific
CitiML implementation requires confirmation from Rajat
and the Data Catalog team.

Do not assume native hierarchy is ready for our use
case until the team confirms it.


